

SQL SERVER ADMINISTRATOR TERMS

1. BASICS
Instance        One running copy of SQL Server (service). It can host many databases.
Database        A container of tables, views, procedures and files.
Schema          A namespace inside a database that groups objects (for example dbo).
T-SQL           Transact-SQL, Microsoft's SQL language.
SSMS            SQL Server Management Studio, the main admin tool.
Collation       Rules for sorting and comparing text (case, accents).
Linked Server   A connection that lets one server query another.
Build / CU      A version number; CU = Cumulative Update (regular patch bundle).
End of Life     The date Microsoft stops releasing security patches for a version.

2. SECURITY
Login           Server-level account that connects to the instance (Layer 1).
User            Database-level account mapped to a login (Layer 2).
Permission      The right to do something on an object, for example SELECT (Layer 3).
Role            A group of permissions (db_owner, db_datareader, sysadmin).
sa              Built-in super-admin SQL login; best disabled if not needed.
sysadmin        Server role with full control of everything.
Orphaned user   A database user whose login no longer exists.
Least privilege Give only the permissions that are actually needed.
TDE             Transparent Data Encryption: encrypts data files at rest.
Audit           Recording who did what and when.

3. FILES AND STORAGE
MDF             Primary data file.
NDF             Secondary data file.
LDF             Transaction log file.
Filegroup       A named group of data files.
Page            8 KB, the basic unit of storage.
Extent          8 pages (64 KB).
Autogrowth      Automatic file growth when a file is full.
tempdb          System database for temporary work; recreated at every restart.
master          System database holding logins and server settings.
msdb            System database for Agent jobs, backup history, replication cleanup.
model           Template used when a new database is created.

4. BACKUP AND RECOVERY
Full backup         Whole database.
Differential        Changes since the last full backup.
Transaction log backup  Log records since the last log backup; enables point-in-time restore.
Recovery model      FULL, SIMPLE or BULK_LOGGED; decides how the log is kept.
Point-in-time restore   Restore to an exact moment, for example just before a mistake.
Restore drill       A test restore on a non-production server to prove backups work.
Checksum            Check that detects damaged backup or data pages.
Retention           How long backups are kept.
RPO                 Recovery Point Objective: how much data loss is acceptable.
RTO                 Recovery Time Objective: how long recovery may take.
DBCC CHECKDB        Checks the database for corruption.

5. TRANSACTIONS AND LOCKING
Transaction     A group of statements that succeed or fail together.
ACID            Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability.
Commit/Rollback Save or undo a transaction.
Lock            Protects data while it is in use.
Blocking        One session waits because another holds a lock.
Deadlock        Two sessions wait on each other; SQL Server kills one.
Isolation level How much one transaction sees of others (READ COMMITTED, SNAPSHOT).
RCSI            Read Committed Snapshot Isolation; readers do not block writers.
Lock escalation Many row locks converted into one table lock.

6. PERFORMANCE
Index           Structure that speeds up lookups.
Clustered index Defines the physical order of the table (one per table).
Nonclustered    Separate lookup structure (many per table).
Fragmentation   Index pages out of order; fixed with REORGANIZE or REBUILD.
Statistics      Data distribution info the optimizer uses to choose a plan.
Execution plan  The steps SQL Server chose to run a query.
Scan / Seek     Reading everything / jumping straight to the rows needed.
Parameter sniffing  A plan built for one parameter value is slow for others.
Missing index   Index SQL Server suggests would help.
Query Store     Built-in history of queries, plans and performance.
DMV             Dynamic Management View: live server information (sys.dm_...).
Wait stats      What the server spends its time waiting on.
PLE             Page Life Expectancy: how long data stays in memory (memory pressure).
MAXDOP          Maximum degree of parallelism (CPU threads per query).
Baseline        Normal performance numbers you compare against.

7. AUTOMATION AND MONITORING
SQL Server Agent    Service that runs scheduled jobs.
Job                 A scheduled task (backup, index maintenance).
Alert               Automatic notification on an error or condition.
Operator            The person or email an alert is sent to.
Database Mail       Feature that sends email from SQL Server.
Maintenance plan    Wizard-built set of routine jobs.
Error log           SQL Server's own log of events and errors.
Extended Events     Lightweight tracing, used to capture deadlocks and slow queries.

8. REPLICATION (your Primary and Subscriber servers)
Publisher           Source server holding the original data.
Distributor         Server holding the distribution database that passes changes along.
Subscriber          Server receiving the data (your reporting server).
Publication         A set of articles published together.
Article             One published object, for example a table.
Subscription        The link between a publication and a subscriber.
Transactional replication  Sends changes continuously, near real time.
Merge replication   Two-way sync with conflict handling.
Snapshot replication Sends a full copy at intervals.
Log Reader Agent    Reads the Publisher's log and writes changes to the distribution database.
Distribution Agent  Delivers the changes to the Subscriber.
Snapshot Agent      Creates the initial copy of the data.
Tracer token        Test marker used to measure replication latency.
Reinitialize        Re-send a full copy to the Subscriber.
Undistributed commands  Changes waiting to be delivered (backlog).

9. HIGH AVAILABILITY AND DR
Failover            Switching to a standby server.
Always On AG        Availability Group: databases kept in sync on several servers.
Log shipping        Log backups copied and restored to a standby server.
Mirroring           Older mirroring feature (deprecated).
DR site             Backup location for disasters.

10. CHANGE AND INCIDENT PROCESS (from your AMC)
CCR                 Change Control Request.
Maintenance window  Low-traffic time agreed for risky work.
Four-eyes principle A second person checks critical changes.
Rollback plan       Steps to undo a failed change.
Sev-1               Highest severity, for example production down.
RCA                 Root Cause Analysis report after an incident.
Known error tracker List of recurring problems and their fixes.
SLA                 Service Level Agreement: promised response and resolution times.
AMC                 Annual Maintenance Contract. 



