KEY SQL SERVER TERMS FOR THE CLIENT DISCUSSION

1. KEEPING THE SYSTEM RUNNING
SQL Server Agent job   An automatic scheduled task (backups, maintenance). If a job fails, nobody notices unless we check.
Alert                  An automatic warning when something goes wrong (disk full, service down), so we act before users complain.
Baseline               Normal performance numbers. We compare today against them to spot problems early.
Autogrowth             Database files growing automatically. Growth trends tell us when you will run out of disk.
End of Life            The date a version stops getting security patches (important for Ubuntu 20.04 and the SQL Server version).

2. PROTECTING YOUR DATA
Full / Differential / Log backup   Whole copy / changes since the last full / continuous record of changes.
Restore drill          A test restore on a non-production server. A backup is only proven when it restores.
RPO                    How much data loss you can accept (for example 15 minutes).
RTO                    How long you can be down while we recover.
DBCC CHECKDB           A health check that finds hidden database corruption early.
Retention              How long backups and logs are kept (your IDRA requirement).

3. SECURITY AND COMPLIANCE
Login / User / Permission   Three layers of access: into the server, into the database, and onto specific data.
sa                     The built-in super-admin account. Best disabled if not needed.
Least privilege        Everyone gets only the access they actually need.
TDE                    Encrypts the database files, so stolen files are unreadable.
Audit trail            Records of who did what and when, ready for IDRA audits.

4. SPEED
Index                  Like a book index; it makes searches fast.
Fragmentation          Index disorder that slows queries; fixed by routine maintenance.
Statistics             Information SQL Server uses to choose the fastest way to run a query. Outdated statistics cause sudden slowness.
Blocking / Deadlock    Users or processes waiting on each other, which makes reports freeze or fail.
Wait stats             Shows what the server is waiting on (disk, memory, locks), so we find the real bottleneck.

5. REPLICATION (Primary to reporting server)
Publisher              Primary server holding the original data.
Distributor            Passes changes along to the reporting server.
Subscriber             Reporting server that receives the data.
Replication latency    The delay before changes show up in reports. This is what users notice.
Undistributed commands Changes waiting to be delivered (a backlog means reports get stale).
Reinitialize           Re-sending a full copy to the reporting server. It is a major action that needs a planned window.

6. DISASTER RECOVERY
Failover               Switching to a standby server when the main one fails.
DR site                Backup location for disasters. The Subscriber is reporting-only, not a DR server.

7. HOW WE WORK (change and incident process)
Maintenance window     Low-traffic time agreed for risky work.
Rollback plan          Steps to undo a change if it goes wrong. Always prepared first.
Four-eyes principle    A second engineer checks critical changes.
Sev-1                  Highest severity: production down. Gets top priority.
RCA                    Root Cause Analysis report within 24 hours of resolution.
SLA                    Agreed response and resolution times.